### --- NOTIFICATIONS ---
### Notification has no endpoints of its own: like dunning, it is an outbox
### relayed to a queue (notification.relay.delay, every 5s by default) and a
### listener that delivers over SMTP. What it does is visible through the
### customer's inbox (Mailpit, http://localhost:8025) and the queue itself
### (ElasticMQ's stats, http://localhost:9325), not through this app's API.
###
### To see all three emails without waiting for real billing/dunning cycles:
###   1. create a product/plan/customer/subscription and force the period
###      closed in psql (below, or see billing.http)
###   2. generate the invoice (below) - this enqueues the invoice-issued
###      email; the relay picks it up within 5s, and it shows up in Mailpit
###      with the PDF attached (rendered on delivery if BillingCycleJob
###      hasn't already run)
###   3. store pm_card_visa_chargeDeclined on the customer (see dunning.http),
###      then run DunningJob (via the cron, or DUNNING_CYCLE_CRON as an
###      environment variable - see dunning.http for why not run.arguments)
###      - the first failure enqueues the payment-failed email
###   4. push dunning_state.attempt_count to 3 and next_attempt_at into the
###      past (see dunning.http), then let the job run again - exhausting
###      the attempts enqueues the subscription-canceled email
###
### Queue depth and the dead-letter queue are visible at
### http://localhost:9325/statistics/queues - a message only ever appears on
### notifications-dlq after 5 failed deliveries (elasticmq.conf's
### maxReceiveCount).


### Auth — acme admin
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/token
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "tenantId": "acme",
  "email": "admin@acme.test",
  "password": "subscriptionhub"
}

> {%
    client.global.set("acmeToken", response.body.token);
%}


### Setup — Product
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/products
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{acmeToken}}

{
  "code": "notification-demo-product",
  "name": "Notification Demo Product"
}

### Setup — Plan (no trial, so the subscription starts ACTIVE)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/plans
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{acmeToken}}

{
  "productCode": "notification-demo-product",
  "code": "notification-demo-plan",
  "name": "Notification Demo Plan",
  "intervalUnit": "MONTH",
  "amountCents": 2999,
  "currency": "USD"
}

### Setup — Customer. Capture the id; this is whose inbox you'll watch.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/customers
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{acmeToken}}

{
  "email": "notification-demo@acme.test",
  "name": "Notification Demo Customer"
}

> {%
    client.global.set("notificationCustomerId", response.body.id);
%}

### Setup — Subscription. Capture the id.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/subscriptions
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{acmeToken}}

{
  "customerId": "{{notificationCustomerId}}",
  "planCode": "notification-demo-plan"
}

> {%
    client.global.set("notificationSubId", response.body.id);
%}

### Force the period closed - psql (or pgAdmin, localhost:8081):
###   UPDATE subscription SET current_period_end = now() - interval '1 day'
###   WHERE id = '<notificationSubId>';


### Invoice-issued email — generates the invoice, which enqueues the email.
### Wait ~5s for the relay, then check Mailpit: http://localhost:8025
### (expects 201; the PDF isn't generated yet at this point - the listener
### renders it itself before attaching, since BillingCycleJob hasn't run)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/subscriptions/{{notificationSubId}}/invoices
Authorization: Bearer {{acmeToken}}

> {%
    client.global.set("notificationInvoiceId", response.body.id);
%}


### --- Watching delivery without opening a browser ---
### These hit Mailpit's own REST API directly, not this application's.

### Every message currently in the inbox (newest last is not guaranteed -
### Mailpit returns newest first)
GET http://localhost:8025/api/v1/messages

### Queue depth for notifications / notifications-dlq
GET http://localhost:9325/statistics/queues
